  "batch_notes": "(1) Duplicate ids: rust/jiff has four property ids that occur more than once because the same test name is used in several files (prop_checked_add_then_sub x2, prop_since_then_add x2, prop_until_then_sub x3, prop_display_fromstr_roundtrip x3; 155 batch entries, 149 distinct ids). aggregate.py keys by id, so only one survives; I emitted one entry per batch occurrence and gave duplicates the same category so the collapse is harmless. (2) Pin policy: following batch-01's convention I tagged pin the properties the README itself calls 'Pinned' (humansize: 7, iso8601: 2), which are carve-outs the broader property gates around; two of the humansize ones (grouped_renderings_are_the_plain_rendering_with_separators, pluralisation_follows_the_printed_value) are really the general property for their domain and carry medium confidence. I did NOT tag pin the general properties that merely carry a KNOWN BUG/KNOWN FAILURE note and an elevated case count (ipnet ipv4_subnets_iterator, ipnet aggregate, iri-string parse_results_respect_type_hierarchy, jiff prop_display_fromstr_roundtrip_fieldwise): they found their bugs as ordinary properties. (3) Recurring shape the rubric does not name: membership-homomorphism checks, op(A,B).contains(x) == A.contains(x) OP B.contains(x) at probe points (iprange x5, ipnet aggregate); I put them under algebraic-law with medium confidence, though model-oracle is arguable. (4) Date/time arithmetic inverse identities (jiff add-then-sub, since-then-add, until-then-sub, x14) went to algebraic-law; roundtrip was reserved for representation/format conversions. (5) 'Round trips' whose inverse is a trivial test-side helper (iri-string percent-decoding, RFC 3986 recomposition) were classified roundtrip with medium confidence. (6) instant-distance: the shared config generator, not any single property, gates around instant-distance/1 (ml capped at 0.95 citing the pin), so the attribution is inferred rather than explicit. (7) Quality: i_overlay's README shows '(no doc comment)' for every property although the tests have detailed doc comments, so the README extraction missed them; humansize's split-by-domain pattern means each bug's direct property is a carve-out, and a direct-attribution count will credit those carve-outs rather than the general property that originally exposed the bug."
